import{_ as a,P as n,d as i,e as o,x as r,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.31 Risk, Disasters, Public Health, Command, and Recovery","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.31 Risk, Disasters, Public Health, Command, and Recovery","xkChars":0,"xkWords":582,"xkLessons":{"D.31":{"title":"D.31 Risk, Disasters, Public Health, Command, and Recovery","lead":"Warnings must cover the full response time, and recovery claims must specify functions, coverage, and support conditions. Demobilizing command does not mean all aspects of life have recovered.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5105–5120, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-31#section-d-31. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘A function genuinely restored under temporary support is not a false recovery.’ Separate hazards, exposure, vulnerability, observation, acceptance of risk, and action. Social conditions do not erase floods or pathogens, and technical estimates alone do not decide who bears risk. Prevention requires comparing harms of action, proportionality, support, and review. It neither expands powers without limit nor requires complete certainty before limited protection is allowed. [T-main]","Complex coupling, reliable organizational practices, latent defenses, system constraints, and adaptations in ordinary tasks each have explanatory scopes. Do not combine different theories into a universal cause of accidents. A temporary workaround that preserves checks differs from eliminating them. What matters is that protective functions and critical distinctions remain tested, not merely that forms have identical formats; proportionate alternatives need professional grounds. [T-main]","The evidential value of no accidents depends on exposure and observation. A process genuinely established after an accident can coexist with an unknown net safety effect and an unsupported branch of feedback learning. Do not erase the process because its net effect is unproven, or declare safety complete because the process exists. [T-main]","Critical slowing down requires particular conditions concerning dynamics, noise, sampling, and bifurcation. Ordinary fluctuations are not automatically signs of collapse. Warnings must reach endpoints able to understand, mobilize, and act in time. Early issuance alone is insufficient: verification, delivery, understanding, dispatch, and protection taking effect all consume time. [T-main]","No harm after a warning may mean the measures worked. If labels are complete and the target fixed, no event after a warning can still be scored against that target. How much harm action prevented requires a causal comparison. Do not prohibit all prediction scoring to prevent misunderstanding, or fill missing offline monitoring records with zero events. [T-main]","Infection, testing, diagnosis, reporting, and hospitalization have different denominators. Changes in beliefs, attendance for testing, and health outcomes do not establish one another. Medical classification, experience, disease course, benefits, and treatment each bear evidential responsibilities. Shared decision-making can improve knowledge without automatically guaranteeing long-term health. This section provides no individual diagnosis or treatment instructions. [T-main]","Emergency command transfer, coordination closeout, withdrawal of special powers, and recovery of everyday life have different timelines. A real function can depend on temporary electricity or continued support. Evaluations must state the conditions and population actually covered, not automatically substitute all predisaster users. A later new shock need not prove an earlier limited conclusion entirely wrong. [T-main]","Specific institutional reference: page 35 of Maryland's April 2023 ICS guide for active assailant incidents separately lists transfer of command, a briefing checklist, and agency closeout. The briefing covers remaining resources, logistics, and documentation. Closeout requires handing over long-term impacts, recovery needs, and unfinished matters, so organizational withdrawal and subsequent recovery are different objects. [P-ICS]","The guide specifies arrangements for a particular scenario; it is not evidence that any incident has fully recovered. Its complete field procedures are not repurposed here as general instructions for the public. The additional distinction is who takes over unfinished work at withdrawal, rather than attaching an overall ‘recovered’ label to closeout. [P-ICS]","A recovery judgment can acknowledge both local reality and gaps. Some households regaining electricity through temporary power is an actual functional recovery, without proving normal supply is repaired or every predisaster user is covered. Broad claims exceeding the evidence must be narrowed; verified local recovery need not be withdrawn wholesale. [T-main]","Connections to Part VI, §8.6, §12.6, §15.3.8, and §16.13 examine dynamics, observation targets, conditional closed loops, and professional boundaries. Urgent protection and long-term evaluation each require proportionate tasks. A complete retrospective explanation must not become a permanently unattainable precondition for action. [T-main]","The eleven entry points are: 31.01, Beck and others on risk, prevention, and uncertainty; 31.02, Perrow, Weick, and others on complex coupling and reliable organization; 31.03, Vaughan and others on normalization of deviance and silence; 31.04, disaster exposure, vulnerability, and resilience; 31.05, warnings, critical slowing down, and response times; 31.06, public-health trust and denominators; 31.07, medicalization and determinants of mental health; 31.08, layers of investigation, accountability, and collective learning; 31.09, medical institutions, ethics, and shared decisions; 31.10, NIMS/ICS handovers and functional degradation; and 31.11, rescue, repair, reconstruction, and recovery boundaries. Names provide navigation without claiming a full reading of the related original works. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Specify hazards, exposure, observation, and who bears risk.","Distinguish an established process, evidence of learning, and net safety effects.","Compare each stage of warning time with the deadline.","Preserve event labels and offline missingness; distinguish prediction scoring from prevented harm.","Recovery records must specify functions, support, and coverage, with handover arrangements at withdrawal."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Establishing a process","leftText":"A verifiable new practice.","rightTitle":"Net safety effect","rightText":"Still requires evidence of consequences; neither can substitute for the other."},{"leftTitle":"Issuing a warning","leftText":"The starting point of information.","rightTitle":"Protection taking effect","rightText":"Requires understanding, dispatch, and timely execution at the endpoint."},{"leftTitle":"No event after a warning","leftText":"An observed label for a given target.","rightTitle":"Prevented harm","rightText":"A counterfactual causal question requiring a separate design."},{"leftTitle":"Closeout","leftText":"Withdrawal of organizational resources.","rightTitle":"Recovery of everyday life","rightText":"Functions and coverage may follow another clock."}],"boundaries":["Source boundary: offline monitoring cannot be recorded as zero events.","Source counterexample: a function can genuinely recover under temporary support; needing support does not make all recovery false.","Teaching counterexample: a warning issued ten minutes early is still insufficient when response requires twelve minutes."],"questions":["Does no accident necessarily mean safety?","Does temporary electricity count as false recovery?","Is ten minutes of warning enough?","Is an established new process with unknown net effects a contradiction?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/figures/d31-clock.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Command transfer and agency closeout in Maryland's 2023 ICS guide","href":"https://aaiwg.maryland.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/35/2021/02/AAIWG-ICS_Field_Operations_Guide-April-2023.pdf"},{"title":"GitLab 2017: service recovered, but not all data were recovered","href":"/en/cases/gitlab-recovery"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Command transfer and agency closeout in Maryland's 2023 ICS guide","text":"Page 35 lists briefings, remaining resources, logistics, and documentation, together with handover of long-term impacts, recovery needs, and unfinished matters at closeout.","sourceLabel":"Maryland Active Assailant Incident Interdisciplinary Work Group: ICS Field Operations Guide for Active Assailant Incidents","sourceUrl":"https://aaiwg.maryland.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/35/2021/02/AAIWG-ICS_Field_Operations_Guide-April-2023.pdf","sourceLocator":"April 2023; page 2 for scope, page 35 for Transfer of Command, Briefing Checklist, and Agency Closeout","boundary":"Limited to institutional arrangements in a guide for specific incidents. It does not prove everyday recovery after any disaster or replace general professional emergency procedures.","href":"https://aaiwg.maryland.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/35/2021/02/AAIWG-ICS_Field_Operations_Guide-April-2023.pdf"},{"title":"GitLab 2017: service recovered, but not all data were recovered","text":"GitLab's February 10, 2017 postmortem records that after the primary database's data directory was accidentally deleted on January 31, the team discovered that daily \`pg_dump\` backups had not been generated because of a version mismatch, and error emails had also been rejected. The database was eventually restored from an LVM snapshot manually made for a test environment about six hours earlier; webhooks were restored separately. GitLab.com became available again after about 18 hours, but approximately six hours of database changes after the snapshot were not recovered. ‘A backup process exists,’ ‘this copy is usable,’ ‘the service is available again,’ and ‘all data were recovered’ must be answered separately to make clear what was restored.","sourceLabel":"GitLab：Postmortem of database outage of January 31，2017-02-10","sourceUrl":"https://about.gitlab.com/blog/postmortem-of-database-outage-of-january-31/","sourceLocator":"Timeline；Broken recovery procedures；Recovering GitLab.com；Data loss impact；Root cause analysis","boundary":"This is GitLab's retrospective account of its own incident, not an independent investigation, and it does not mention recovery criteria announced beforehand. It is used here only to distinguish the scope of recovery claims, not as a formal repair test.","href":"/en/cases/gitlab-recovery"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.31","start_line":5196,"end_line":5211,"startLine":5196,"endLine":5211}],"outputId":"section-d-31","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: lead time must cover the whole chain","text":"Artificial durations are two minutes for verification, one for delivery, two for understanding, three for dispatch, and four for protection to take effect: twelve minutes in series. A ten-minute lead is two minutes short; a fifteen-minute lead leaves three minutes, still conditional on each stage completing as assumed.","assumptions":"Artificial strictly serial stages, fixed durations, and no other delay; this is not an operational threshold for a particular disaster.","boundary":"Real stages may run in parallel or face resource constraints; professional assessment at the site is required."},{"title":"Teaching construction: support conditions and coverage","text":"An artificial area had 100 households before a disaster, and temporary electricity has now been restored to 60. One can say that electricity is restored to these 60 under temporary support, covering 60% of the predisaster households, but not that all are restored. If only 80 households are currently registered, 60/80 = 75% uses a different denominator and cannot replace 60/100.","assumptions":"An artificial common service standard and verifiable identities; no real disaster area is involved.","boundary":"This proves neither long-term stability nor recovery of other aspects of life, and does not show that unregistered households do not exist."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: offline monitoring cannot be recorded as zero events.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: a function can genuinely recover under temporary support; needing support does not make all recovery false.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: a warning issued ten minutes early is still insufficient when response requires twelve minutes.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does no accident necessarily mean safety?","answer":"Exposure and observation are needed; missingness cannot count as zero.","basis":"Evidence."},{"question":"Does temporary electricity count as false recovery?","answer":"A verified function can genuinely recover; state the support and coverage.","basis":"Conditions."},{"question":"Is ten minutes of warning enough?","answer":"It depends on the whole chain's duration. This artificial example requires twelve minutes, so ten is insufficient.","basis":"Timing."},{"question":"Is an established new process with unknown net effects a contradiction?","answer":"No contradiction: these are outcomes at different layers.","basis":"Layers."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/figures/d31-clock.png","alt":"Artificial serial durations: 2 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 12; a ten-minute lead is insufficient.","caption":"Teaching construction."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/figures/d31-coverage.png","alt":"Artificial recovery for 60 households: 60% coverage against 100 predisaster households, or 75% with the current denominator of 80.","caption":"Teaching construction."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/figures/d31-handover.png","alt":"The specific 2023 ICS guide requires unfinished recovery matters to be handed over during command transfer and closeout.","caption":"This is not proof of recovery outcomes at a site."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures distinguish warning timing, recovery coverage, and command transfer.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No specific public-health intervention or individual treatment was evaluated.","Actual data on closeout and long-term recovery for a particular incident were not checked."],"taskId":"S-D-31","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.31, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 5017–5032","claim_support":["Risk evidence, warning clocks, learning, medical boundaries, withdrawal and recovery, and eleven entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ICS","title":"Maryland Active Assailant Incident Interdisciplinary Work Group: ICS Field Operations Guide for Active Assailant Incidents","url":"https://aaiwg.maryland.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/35/2021/02/AAIWG-ICS_Field_Operations_Guide-April-2023.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023-04","locator":"April 2023; page 2 for scope, page 35 for Transfer of Command, Briefing Checklist, and Agency Closeout","claim_support":["Transfer of command requires briefings on resources, logistics, and documentation; closeout requires handing over unfinished matters and long-term impacts or recovery needs."],"limits":["The lesson author read the specified pages, without claiming to read all 83. The guide concerns active assailant incidents and prescribes arrangements; it is not empirical evidence that everyday life recovered after a particular incident."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"gitlab-2017-postmortem","title":"GitLab：Postmortem of database outage of January 31","url":"https://about.gitlab.com/blog/postmortem-of-database-outage-of-january-31/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2017-02-10","locator":"Timeline；Broken recovery procedures；Recovering GitLab.com；Data loss impact；Root cause analysis","claim_support":["The daily logical backup had not been generated; recovery instead used an LVM snapshot about six hours old; webhooks were restored separately; database modifications from the subsequent roughly six hours could not be recovered."],"limits":["GitLab's retrospective account of its own incident, not an independent investigation; it mentions no recovery criteria published beforehand and is not used as the basis for a formal repair test. The backup and snapshot status is as of February 10, 2017."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.31 Risk, Disasters, Public Health, Command, and Recovery; Chinese source lines 5105–5120; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/source/S-D-31.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-31/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4c97d84ba60bb46056f24813078cbc4bc4303a1d70981be3f456fbf705ce1e78","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-31.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-31.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-31.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,f){const t=n("SectionLesson");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-31" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.31</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Risk, Disasters, Public Health, Command, and Recovery <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-31" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.31 Risk, Disasters, Public Health, Command, and Recovery {#section-d-31}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §6, §8.6, §12.6, §15.3.8, and §16.13. Research entries cover 31.01–31.11.</p><p>Hazard sources, exposure, vulnerability, observation, acceptance of risk, and action are distinct. Social conditions cannot erase the intensity of a flood or the presence of a pathogen. Technical estimates alone cannot determine who should bear the risk. Preventive measures should be compared in terms of the harms of action itself, proportionality, feasible support, and provision for review. Uncertainty does not justify an unlimited expansion of authority, and limited protective action need not wait for complete certainty.</p><p>Complex coupling, reliable organizational practices, latent lines of defense, system constraints, and routine task adaptations each have their own explanatory scope. A temporary workaround that retains checks is distinct from removing checks. Whether checks have been retained depends on whether the protective function and task-critical distinctions are still tested. The format can differ. A substitute verification procedure appropriate to the task must have a professional basis. The evidentiary value of observing no incidents depends on exposure and observation. A new process may actually have been established after an incident even if its net effect on safety is still undetermined and one branch of feedback-mediated learning still lacks support.</p><p>Critical slowing down applies only under the corresponding conditions of dynamics, noise, sampling, and bifurcation. Arbitrary fluctuations cannot be taken as signs of impending collapse. An early warning must reach an endpoint where the warning can be understood, a response can be organized, and protective action can be completed in time. An absence of harm after a warning may mean the measures worked. Infection, testing, confirmed diagnosis, reporting, and hospitalization have different denominators. Changes in beliefs, actually presenting for testing, and health outcomes cannot be inferred from one another.</p><p>Medical classification, lived experience, the course of illness, provision of benefits, and treatment choices each carry their own evidentiary burden. Shared decision-making can improve knowledge and does not automatically guarantee long-term health. Emergency command handoffs, coordinated demobilization, the end of special powers, and recovery of living conditions have different clocks. Functional recovery achieved with temporary support is real. A later shock does not necessarily show that the earlier conclusion was entirely wrong. Only recovery claims that exceed the evidence already available need to be narrowed accordingly.</p><p>Warning lead time must cover verification, reaching recipients, comprehension, mobilization, and the time it takes for protective measures to take effect. If labels are complete and the target has been fixed, the absence of a post-warning event can still be scored against that target. Estimating how much harm action prevented requires a separate causal comparison. Periods when monitoring is offline cannot be coded as zero events. Recovery of function with temporary power or ongoing support can be genuine recovery. The assessment must retain the support conditions and the population actually covered. It cannot automatically be extended to all pre-disaster users.</p><p>Research topics. 31.01 Risk society, prevention, and uncertainty (Ulrich Beck and others). 31.02 Normal accidents and high-reliability organizations (Perrow, Weick, and others). 31.03 Normalization of deviance and organizational silence (Diane Vaughan and others). 31.04 Disaster vulnerability, exposure, and resilience. 31.05 Early warnings, critical slowing down, and response clocks. 31.06 Epidemics, public health, and social trust. 31.07 Medicalization, mental health, and determinants of health. 31.08 Incident investigation, accountability, and collective learning. 31.09 Health care institutions, clinical ethics, and shared decision-making. 31.10 NIMS, ICS, emergency command, and functional degradation. 31.11 Rescue, repair, reconstruction, and false recovery.</p></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"D.31"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=a(d,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
